WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.028 --> 00:00:02.002
Welcome to Poker Bot Arena.

2
00:00:02.002 --> 00:00:09.242
In this tutorial, you'll write a poker bot from scratch in Kicker, the arena's rule based language.

3
00:00:09.242 --> 00:00:12.825
Then you'll test it, debug it, and watch it play.

4
00:00:13.177 --> 00:00:15.427
There's no machine learning here.

5
00:00:15.427 --> 00:00:21.020
A bot is just a list of rules that you can read from top to bottom, like this one.

6
00:00:21.371 --> 00:00:23.920
Everything happens in the Bot Lab.

7
00:00:24.273 --> 00:00:28.670
On the left are your bots, and the eight built in personalities.

8
00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:30.937
In the middle is the code editor.

9
00:00:30.937 --> 00:00:33.961
And on the right are tools to test your bot.

10
00:00:34.311 --> 00:00:36.319
Press New to start a bot.

11
00:00:36.319 --> 00:00:39.774
You get a small example that already works.

12
00:00:40.128 --> 00:00:41.567
Here's the key idea.

13
00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:49.411
Every time it's your turn, the program runs from top to bottom, and the first action it reaches is your move.

14
00:00:49.411 --> 00:00:54.665
If it reaches no action, it checks when that's free, and folds otherwise.

15
00:00:55.017 --> 00:00:56.429
Let's write our own.

16
00:00:56.429 --> 00:01:01.230
Clear the editor, then give the bot a name, an avatar, and a colour.

17
00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:06.525
These lines are optional, but they make your bot easy to spot at the table.

18
00:01:06.878 --> 00:01:09.513
Now the rules for before the flop.

19
00:01:09.513 --> 00:01:15.481
The words, on preflop, start a block that only runs before the flop is dealt.

20
00:01:15.837 --> 00:01:18.334
Each rule reads like a sentence.

21
00:01:18.334 --> 00:01:21.611
When something is true, then do something.

22
00:01:21.611 --> 00:01:27.385
Our best hands, tens or better and big aces, raise to three times the bet.

23
00:01:27.737 --> 00:01:32.696
Decent hands, in the top twenty percent, call when it's cheap.

24
00:01:33.045 --> 00:01:34.902
Everything else folds.

25
00:01:34.902 --> 00:01:37.773
And the word end closes the block.

26
00:01:38.125 --> 00:01:39.755
Now, after the flop.

27
00:01:39.755 --> 00:01:42.934
Kicker checks your code while you type.

28
00:01:42.934 --> 00:01:46.358
Watch what happens if I misspell strength.

29
00:01:46.714 --> 00:01:48.994
The line turns red straight away.

30
00:01:48.994 --> 00:01:54.452
The problems list says it doesn't know that name, and suggests the one I meant.

31
00:01:54.803 --> 00:01:58.662
Fix the spelling, and the bot is ready to play again.

32
00:01:59.012 --> 00:02:03.920
Strength is our chance of beating one random hand, from zero to one.

33
00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:07.385
Very strong hands bet three quarters of the pot.

34
00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:15.268
Equity is our chance against everyone still in the hand, and pot odds is the share of the final pot we'd be paying.

35
00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:19.457
When equity beats the price, calling wins money in the long run.

36
00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.320
And a little bluff.

37
00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:28.078
When nobody has bet, one time in five, we bet half the pot, and say something cheeky.

38
00:02:28.429 --> 00:02:32.313
Anything else, check, or fold to a bet.

39
00:02:32.664 --> 00:02:34.878
Save with control S.

40
00:02:35.234 --> 00:02:38.083
Now let's see what the bot really does.

41
00:02:38.083 --> 00:02:42.832
Try a spot deals a random hand, and plays it until it's our turn.

42
00:02:43.183 --> 00:02:48.583
It shows our cards, the board, every value the bot can see, and its decision.

43
00:02:48.583 --> 00:02:52.511
The line that made the decision lights up in the editor.

44
00:02:52.862 --> 00:02:53.963
Is it any good?

45
00:02:53.963 --> 00:03:00.205
The test tab plays a thousand hands against the opponents you pick, in a few seconds.

46
00:03:00.558 --> 00:03:07.781
This is our score, in big blinds won per hundred hands, with a full leaderboard and a chip graph underneath.

47
00:03:08.133 --> 00:03:11.736
Numbers are nice, but it's more fun to watch.

48
00:03:11.736 --> 00:03:17.021
In Simulate, seat Night Owl with the built in bots, and press run.

49
00:03:17.374 --> 00:03:24.836
You can watch at up to sixteen times speed, pause, step through every action, and show everyone's cards.

50
00:03:24.836 --> 00:03:29.356
Every hand goes in the log, and the standings update as you go.

51
00:03:29.707 --> 00:03:31.996
Let's watch a few hands.

52
00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:40.861
That's the whole loop.

53
00:03:40.861 --> 00:03:43.249
Write, check, test, and watch.

54
00:03:43.249 --> 00:03:53.996
Open any built in bot to see how it's made, share yours with a link, or open an online table, and let your friends' bots take on yours.

55
00:03:53.996 --> 00:03:55.907
Good luck at the tables!
